THIRD-PARTY WORKS COMMITTED IN THIS REPOSITORY
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Almost every dependency of this project is resolved at build time and is not
redistributed here. Three kinds are, and are therefore listed: fonts, committed
as binaries; component source copied into the tree rather than installed; and
dataset snapshots committed so that nothing in the build has to fetch them.

Wikidata
  Structured data, dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal
  https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/
  https://www.wikidata.org
  The CSV files under tools/catalogue/data/ are frozen results of the SPARQL
  queries committed beside them, retrieved 2026-08-11 from
  https://query.wikidata.org/sparql. They are the source deploy/catalogue.json's
  product titles, descriptions, manufacturers and airport codes are derived
  from. CC0 requires no attribution; this entry is provenance, and it records
  which committed files are not this project's own work.

  Only Wikidata's structured data is CC0. Media on Wikimedia Commons is not, and
  none is used: every image file this repository ships is drawn by it, by hand
  under frontend/public/images/catalogue or by tools/catalogue/mark.go under the
  derived/ directory beside them.

  "Ships" is doing work in that sentence and did not have to before issue #300.
  Under `make demo-live` the page also displays photographs that are not in this
  repository and are not this project's: the browser loads them from DummyJSON's
  CDN. Nothing about the 63 committed offers changed — see the DummyJSON entry
  below for what is taken and on what terms.

DummyJSON
  Copyright (c) Muhammad Ovi (Owais)
  MIT License
  https://github.com/Ovi/DummyJSON
  backend/internal/roles/merchant/shop/data/dummyjson-products.json is one
  recorded response from https://dummyjson.com, retrieved 2026-08-12 — see
  PROVENANCE.md beside it for the exact request. It is committed so that no test
  has to make that request, which AGENTS.md's hard rule 4 forbids outright.

  It is the *only* place this shop appears in a build that is not asked for one:
  `cmd/merchant -catalogue-live dummyjson`, which only `make demo-live` passes,
  fetches from the live host at start-up. `make demo`, `make check`, `make
  vectors` and CI reach it never.

  What DummyJSON serves is placeholder data published as placeholder data.
  Nothing in the recording is anybody's real product listing and nothing was
  scraped.

  IMPORTANT: **images are taken from this shop**, and this entry is where that
  is recorded. Until issue #300 none was, and this paragraph said so — a fetched
  offer's picture used to be a mark this project drew from the offer's
  identifier. What happens now is that the merchant asks for the `thumbnail`
  column, puts the URL the shop returns into the offer it builds, and serves
  that offer to the browser. So under `make demo-live` a viewer's browser
  fetches one photograph per fetched offer from https://cdn.dummyjson.com —
  194 of them in the response recorded on 2026-08-12 — and shows them in the
  same table as the 63 offers whose pictures this project drew. No image file is
  copied into this repository and none is served from it; what is committed is
  the recorded response above, which since that date carries those URLs as text.
  Where the shop supplies no usable thumbnail, the drawn mark is still what an
  offer shows.

  Three things follow that the licence line above does not settle, and a reader
  who thinks this was the wrong call should be able to say so from them:

  1. **The MIT licence covers DummyJSON's software** — the server published in
     that repository. It says nothing about the provenance of the product
     photographs that server hands out, and this project has not established
     where those pictures came from or who holds copyright in them. They are
     displayed from the shop's own host, unmodified, in a demonstration that
     sells nothing and moves no money. That is a narrower position than "the
     pictures are MIT licensed", which nothing here is in a position to claim.

  2. **The page now loads from a host this project does not control.** That is
     a dependency this repository refused twice before — issue #215 ended four
     broken images by requiring every picture to be a file it ships, and issue
     #243 kept the rule for fetched offers by drawing marks instead. The cost
     is the one those issues named: when cdn.dummyjson.com is slow, unreachable
     or has moved a path, the fetched half of the product table shows broken
     images, and both img tags carry `alt=""`, so it shows them silently.
     Nothing else acquired a network dependency — the build, the tests and CI
     reach no network, and this one is the browser's, under `make demo-live`
     alone. A Content-Security-Policy for the frontend would need

         img-src 'self' data: https://cdn.dummyjson.com

     There is no CSP in this repository today; that line is what one would have
     to say. backend/internal/roles/merchant/mark.go carries the argument for
     making this trade, and what was given up to make it.

     **The host in that line is an observation, not a rule the code keeps.**
     What the merchant checks is `https://`, a host after it, and no whitespace
     or quote — never which host. cdn.dummyjson.com is where all 194 thumbnails
     in the recorded response point, and it is therefore where a run today sends
     a browser; a shop that answered with a different host would be believed,
     and the line above would be the wrong policy rather than an unenforced one.
     That is deliberate — a merchant reads its stock through an interface and
     does not know which shop answered, so naming one host would put the shop's
     identity in the wrong package — and it is written here rather than only in
     mark.go, because "which third-party hosts does this page talk to" is a
     question a reader comes to this file for, and the honest answer is
     "whichever ones `-catalogue-live` fetched from, one shop's word for it".

     What bounds it in practice is one directory along: `cmd/merchant`'s
     `-catalogue-live` refuses every value but `dummyjson`, so the shop whose
     word is taken is not itself configurable at run time.

  3. **A free service now carries this repository's demonstration traffic.**
     Not a licence question and not a copyright one, which is why it is its own
     point: every page view under `make demo-live` fetches up to 194 images from
     a shop that charges nothing and promises nothing. The columns asked for in
     the request are kept to the nine actually used, for the same reason — see
     shop/data/PROVENANCE.md — but that courtesy was cheap and this one is not.
     Anybody running this at any scale, or building on it, should be hosting
     those images themselves.

